ill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/5)**: ingest DONE. NEXT: scan p254–255 → ONE rebuild; import .ris; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n- **DEV→GitHub (9/9)**: DONE. 128 folders on `erash11`. DexaProject off GitHub→Drive; 18 repos now private. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5861 chars, updated 2026-09-10 12:00)\n## Today\nThursday, September 10, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n**Context:** Continuing the 2026-09-09 GitHub consolidation of `~/DEV` — finishing the thin-repo conversion, writing the Windows handoff, auditing 21 public repos for exposed athlete data, and triaging 36 Windows/Mac conflict files.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked what actually needed doing about the 21 public repos and the 36 conflict files, rather than accepting the flagged counts at face value. Reading inside all 21 repos showed most privacy flags were false alarms — only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real named athletes with force plate data (Devin Turner, Kamauryn Morgan) plus real rosters. `Practice_PlayCounts` looked risky but its roster is fake sample data.\n- User's directive on visibility: \"Flip everything private that isn't going to make a website go dark.\"\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- `~/DEV` is now the thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 files: the 8 router `.md` files plus `.gitignore`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md`. Zero project folders. `.gitignore` starts with `/*` (block-all, then allow the `.md` files back) — that line is what keeps it thin and must not be removed.\n- `CLAUDE.md` in DEV was deliberately left as a workspace map, NOT converted to an `@AGENTS.md` pointer. Rationale recorded inside `AGENTS.md` so a future pass doesn't \"fix\" it.\n- 18 repos flipped private; 3 left public because each runs a live site: `SportScienceResearchRepo` (GH Pages), `Travel_Map` (GH Pages), `JumpReporting` (Vercel).\n- `SportScienceResearchRepo` left untouched — ~4,700 research PDFs published publicly is a copyright question, not a privacy one, and the call is the user's.\n- Memory updated (Tier A `github-consolidation` + working memory): Windows box is ACTIVE, not retired 8/14; personal site is pushed; DexaProject relocated to Google Drive.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **GitHub Pages is not the only way a repo backs a live site.** The first safety check cleared `JumpReporting` as \"no Pages site, safe to flip\" — it actually deploys to Vercel. Caught only on a second pass that looked for external deploys.\n- **A 404 body can read as a hit.** The initial site-liveness check had a bug where 404 responses were being counted as live sites; had to re-run with proper status-code handling before trusting the results.\n- **Repo names mislead on sensitivity.** Repos that *mention* athletes in code (`JumpReporting`, `VALD_analytics`, `PT_Dash`) carry no athlete data files; the real exposure was in two repos that didn't stand out by name. Reading inside is the only reliable filter.\n- **The 36 conflict files collapsed to 4 real ones** once diffed: 26 differed only by CRLF line endings, 6 were `graphify-out/`/`portfolio.json` where Mac is simply newer.\n- **\"Mac wins\" is a dangerous default for append-only logs.** `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` — the Mac-wins rule kept the *smaller* file; Windows holds 324 forecast rows (back to 2026-08-26) that exist nowhere else. Newer ≠ more complete.\n- Private repos can still break downstream publishing: `printing-press-library` publishes to npm, `GameONPaper` pushes Docker containers. Flipping back is instant and lossless if a build fails.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Delete `erash11/DexaProject`** — still on GitHub with the patient CSV. Needs `gh auth refresh -h github.com -s delete_repo` first, then `gh repo delete erash11/DexaProject`.\n- **Merge the CFB forecast log** so the 324 Windows-only rows aren't lost — offered, not yet done.\n- **Open 3 Word/Excalidraw files** in `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` and compare by hand — the DRAFT is 1.0 MB on Windows vs 0.4 MB on Mac.\n- Delete the 32 safe conflict files (26 CRLF-only + 6 Mac-newer) — zero risk.\n- Decide on `SportScienceResearchRepo`'s ~4,700 public PDFs (copyright).\n- Windows-side work is queued in `~/DEV/docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md` (10 sections); user has the paste-ready prompt for the Windows LLM, including \"do not skip section 5, report back on section 10.\"\n\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA","sessionId":"5f2ac916-6e52-43f6-bc78-4e7231c4afa0","version":"2.1.268","gitBranch":"main"}
